# The approved identity of the Silesia corpus archive: repository-controlled,
# committed, and reviewed like code.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# AAP 0.8.4 states the port's throughput targets against the Silesia corpus, and
# 0.6.4.4 requires that no build or test step ever reach the network to get it.
# Those two together leave exactly one honest arrangement: the corpus is fetched
# deliberately, out of band, and its identity is fixed *here* rather than by
# whoever happens to run the fetch.
#
# Before this file existed, `fetch_silesia.sh` shipped its built-in digest as the
# literal string `UNPINNED`, so a release run's acceptance measurement depended on
# an environment variable somebody had to remember to set.  A required check could
# be green having verified nothing, and the AAP's own acceptance corpus could be
# absent from a passing release with no signal anywhere.  The digest below is the
# fix: it is in the repository, it moves only through a reviewed commit, and both
# `fetch_silesia.sh` and `.github/workflows/rust.yml` read it from here rather than
# carrying their own copy.
#
# WHAT IT PINS, AND WHY EACH FIELD
#
# The archive digest alone would authenticate the download and nothing else.  The
# other fields are the resource ceilings the fetch enforces *before* it has
# written 68 MB and *before* it expands that into 202 MiB -- an integrity check
# that runs after the disk is full has protected nothing (CWE-400).  Every bound
# below is the measured truth about the approved archive, so a mirror that serves
# something larger is refused on the size, not merely on the digest, and is
# refused early.
#
# HOW TO READ IT
#
# Plain `key=value`, one per line, `#` comments, no quoting, no continuation, no
# substitution.  It is parsed by a shell script with `case`/`read` and by a YAML
# workflow with `grep`, so it must stay parseable by both without either needing a
# parser.  Do not add a field type that `key=value` cannot express.
#
# HOW TO CHANGE IT
#
# Run `./fetch_silesia.sh --pin-status` to see what is pinned, and
# `./fetch_silesia.sh --print-pin <dir-or-archive>` to compute the block a new
# archive would need.  Change `url` and `sha256` together and never separately: a
# digest without the URL it was taken from asserts nothing about where the bytes
# came from.  Expect the size and member fields to move with them.

# Where the approved archive was retrieved from.  HTTPS only; `fetch_silesia.sh`
# refuses every other scheme fail-closed, before it reads or creates anything.
url=https://sun.aei.polsl.pl/~sdeor/corpus/silesia.zip

# SHA-256 of the archive exactly as served by the URL above, lowercase hex.
# Verified on 2026-08-12 against a fresh retrieval.
sha256=0626e25f45c0ffb5dc801f13b7c82a3b75743ba07e3a71835a41e3d9f63c77af

# Size of that archive in bytes.  Enforced as a transfer ceiling before the
# download starts (from the server's advertised length) and again as an exact
# equality after it finishes, so a truncated or padded transfer is caught without
# hashing 68 MB first.
archive_bytes=68182744

# How many regular files the archive expands to.  Upstream publishes twelve, one
# per data type, and `members` in `fetch_silesia.sh` names them.
member_count=12

# Total expanded size in bytes: 202.1 MiB.  Enforced against the sum of the
# archive's own member headers *before* extraction, so an archive claiming to
# expand to more than the approved corpus is refused without writing a byte.
expanded_bytes=211938580

# The largest single member, `mozilla` at 48.8 MiB.  A per-member ceiling as well
# as a total one, because one member claiming 200 MiB inside a total that happens
# to add up is still an archive nobody approved.
max_member_bytes=51220480

# Expanded bytes divided by archive bytes, to two decimal places: 211938580 /
# 68182744 = 3.11.  Enforced with a margin as a compression-ratio ceiling, which
# is the bound that catches a zip bomb whose declared sizes are honest but
# enormous relative to what was downloaded.
compression_ratio=3.11
